summary.value:
This paper addresses the limitation of GNNs in capturing long-range atomic interactions and distinguishing crystal structures due to local receptive fields. To resolve this, the authors propose PRDNet, which integrates graph embeddings with a "pseudo-particle" ray diffraction module. By using learnable form factors rather than fixed tabulated ones, the model generates symmetry-invariant diffraction patterns that effectively encode global periodic information.

Strength:
(1) Innovatively integrates physical diffraction laws with deep learning via learnable "pseudo-particles"; 
(2) Mathematically guaranteed to maintain strict $E(3)$ invariance while effectively capturing global long-range periodic interactions;
(3) Comprehensive experiments and ablation studies to verify the effectiveness of the proposed model.

Weakness:
(1) The initial version lacked a clear technical distinction from existing methods; 
(2) There was initial uncertainty regarding the module's compatibility and effectiveness when integrated with different GNN backbones;
(3) Some SOTA baselines are not incorporated.
